**Technique 1 — additive odd-length construction.** Take a small cell (your example: root–♭3–5–6 = A–C–E–F♯). Build a longer line by concatenating *uneven* sub-groups of it — 4, then the first 3, then 4 again (= 11), then a rotation like E–F♯–A–C (= 15), then +1 to close (= 16). All straight 16ths, no rests. The trick: the melodic group-boundaries land at positions 1, 5, 8, 12, 16 — **not** on the beats (1, 5, 9, 13) — so within a simple 1-bar unit the accents are off-grid, and across 1–2 bars the larger sequence never divides evenly by 4. Simple locally, hypnotic-but-intentional globally — and it collapses into "random" if you run it longer than ~2 bars before realigning.

**Technique 2 — displace-and-rewind.** Take a running ostinato; at a chosen point open a small pause (shift the rest of the bar forward an 8th/a couple 16ths = a breath), let the displaced version run ~1 beat, then "rewind" — drop the equivalent notes so it snaps back onto the grid by the barline. Next bar, do it at a *different* point, so the breaths migrate — a faint temporal symmetry with almost no literal repetition. Works with staccato 16ths *or* held/legato notes (where the pattern "gets lost" more).

